Values, types, and expressions.

What is covered. This notebook walks through how Scala 3 names a value, what type that value gets, and how those named values combine into the larger expressions that make up a program. Val versus var. Lazy val. The type hierarchy from any down to nothing. Type inference and the three places you should still write the type explicitly. The numeric types and the silent gotchas that come from sitting on top of the java virtual machine's primitives. String and the three built-in interpolators. Boolean, char, and the strange residents unit, null, and nothing. Type aliases. The single biggest mental shift in this notebook, which is that if, for, and blocks are expressions that have a value. The one exception, which is while. Operators as method calls. And type ascription.

Val versus var.

Two ways to name something in Scala. They look almost identical and behave very differently.

A val is a single-assignment binding. Once given a value, the name cannot be re-pointed at a different value. This is the Scala default — reach for it first.

A var is a mutable variable. The name can be reassigned to a different value over its lifetime. Use it deliberately when you actually need a counter, an accumulator, or a state machine — not as a reflex from another language.

The bias is real. Idiomatic Scala uses val for the overwhelming majority of bindings. New code that reaches for var should make you ask — is there a way to express this as a transformation that produces a new value, rather than a mutation that updates an old one.

In code, picture two declarations. Val pi equals three point one four one five nine — this binding is locked. If you write pi equals three point one four on a later line, the compiler refuses, with the error reassignment to val. Var counter equals zero — this binding is mutable. You can write counter equals counter plus one twice and end up at two. But even with var, you cannot change the type. If you bind var n to ten, n is inferred to be an integer. You can later assign ninety-nine to it because that is also an integer. You cannot assign the string ten to it, because the type was fixed at the first assignment.

Two subtleties worth pinning down now, because both come back later.

Val locks the binding, not the value it points at. If you bind a val to a mutable object — say a scala collection mutable array buffer, or a java array list, or a Scala array — the binding is fixed but the contents of the object can still change in place. We will see this concretely when we meet array in notebook four. The takeaway. Immutability is a property of the type, not of the keyword val.

The right-hand side is evaluated immediately. When you write val x equals expensive of parentheses, expensive of parentheses runs at the point of the val declaration, not the first time you read x. If you want lazy evaluation, that is lazy val, which is the next section.

Lazy val — computed at most once, on first read.

A lazy val is a binding whose right-hand side is not evaluated when the declaration is reached. It runs the first time the name is actually used, and the result is then cached for every subsequent read.

Two common reasons to reach for it. The computation is expensive and might not be needed at all in some code paths. Or the computation depends on something that is not ready yet at declaration time but will be later.

Behind the scenes the compiler generates a private field for the cached result and a thread-safe synchronized initialization block. So lazy val is safe to share across threads, at the cost of a small first-access overhead.

In code, picture two declarations side by side. One val called eager, of type integer, runs a side effect that bumps a counter and prints the word eager evaluated, and returns forty-two. One lazy val called lazy-one, of type integer, does the same kind of work — bumps the counter and prints lazy evaluated — but returns ninety-nine. Then on the next line, you print the side-effect counter, which is one. Only eager ran. The lazy val has not been touched. Then you print the value of lazy-one for the first time, and you see the side-effect output appear, and then the value ninety-nine. Then you print lazy-one a second time, and you see only the value, no side effect — it was cached. Finally the side-effect counter reads two.

The type hierarchy.

Every value in Scala is an instance of a type, and every type sits somewhere in a single rooted hierarchy. Knowing the shape of that hierarchy explains a lot of subsequent behavior — what the compiler accepts, what null does, what an empty list's element type is, why throw can appear in a position that expects a value.

Picture a tree with one root at the top called any. Any has two children. The first child is any-val, which is the parent of every value type backed by a java virtual machine primitive — integer, long, double, boolean, char, byte, short, float, plus the strange one-value type unit. The second child is any-ref, which is the parent of every reference type — every class, including standard library types like string, list, and map, plus every class you define yourself. Any-ref is exactly java lang object under the hood.

Below the tree, two more types sit specially. Null is the type of the literal null. It is a subtype of every reference type. You can assign null to a string — though you should not — but you cannot assign null to an integer, because null is not a subtype of any-val. Nothing is the bottom of the entire hierarchy. It is a subtype of every type, including the value types. Nothing has no values. It is the type of expressions that never return, like throw of a new exception. We will use this idea in notebook six when we meet option.

In code, picture three vals bound to any. One holds the integer forty-two. One holds the string forty two. One holds a list of three integers. All three type-check, because every value in Scala is an any. Then picture a function called crash-and-burn that takes a message and returns integer. Inside, the body is just throw of a new runtime exception. The compiler is happy with this, because throw has type nothing, and nothing is a subtype of integer. So an expression that never returns satisfies any declared return type. Then picture a val called maybe-name, declared as string, assigned to null. The program compiles, prints the literal text null, and any method call you make on it crashes with a null pointer exception. Idiomatic Scala uses option, from notebook six, instead.

Type inference — what the compiler does for free.

Scala is statically typed, but the compiler does most of the typing work for you. For a val, the compiler reads the right-hand side, computes its type, and uses that as the type of the binding. No annotation needed.

Most of the code you read and write will look like that — no explicit types in sight. You write val a equals forty-two, and the compiler infers integer. Val b equals three point one four, inferred double. Val c equals the string scala, inferred string. Val d equals list of one two three, inferred list of integer. Val e equals the tuple one, the string one, one point zero, inferred as a three-tuple of integer, string, double.

But there are three places where you should write the type explicitly even when inference would succeed.

On public method signatures. A method's parameter and return types are part of its contract. Hide the types and a downstream change to the body silently changes the type the world depends on. Always annotate the return type of a def that other code calls.

When inference picks something wider than you intended. A common one. Val xs equals list of one, two point zero, the string three — the compiler infers a wide upper bound, almost never what you wanted. Writing val xs colon list of any equals the same expression makes the intent explicit. Or better, restructure to avoid the heterogeneous list.

When the right-hand side is recursive or mutually recursive. Recursive defs need a return type annotation to break the inference cycle. Without it, the compiler errors with recursive method needs result type.

In code, picture three examples. One — a public method called normalise-email, taking a raw string, returning string, body trims the raw input and lowercases it. Two — a recursive method called factorial, taking n of type long, returning long, body is the standard if n less than or equal to one then one, else n times factorial of n minus one. Without the return type annotation, this would not compile. Three — a method called stats, taking a list of integer, returning a tuple of integer and double, body binds total to the sum and mean to total divided by length, and returns the pair. The local vals inside the body do not need type annotations.

The honest rule. Let inference handle local vals in method bodies, but annotate every public-facing signature.

The numeric types.

Scala's numeric types are thin wrappers over java virtual machine primitives. Their sizes and ranges are exactly what the java virtual machine provides.

Byte is eight bits, ranging negative one twenty eight to one twenty seven. Short is sixteen bits, with the corresponding wider range. Integer is thirty-two bits, ranging roughly plus or minus two point one billion. This is the default for an integer literal — when you write forty-two, you get an integer. Long is sixty-four bits, ranging roughly plus or minus nine point two quintillion. To write a long literal, add the suffix capital L. Float is thirty-two-bit single-precision i-e-e-e seven fifty-four floating point — the suffix lowercase f. Double is sixty-four-bit double-precision floating point — the default for decimal literals. Big-int is arbitrary precision, unbounded but slower and on the heap, written as big-int of a quoted string. Big-decimal is arbitrary precision decimal, with exact decimal arithmetic, also written as big-decimal of a quoted string.

Three things you have to remember when working with these.

Default literal types. An integer literal like forty-two is an integer, not a long. A decimal literal like three point one four is a double, not a float. Add the suffix capital L or lowercase f to override.

Overflow is silent. Adding past the maximum of an integer wraps around to a large negative number. The compiler will not warn you. Reach for long when the magnitudes can grow, or big-int when they can grow without bound.

Floating point is not decimal. Zero point one plus zero point two produces zero point three zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero zero four in a double, exactly as it does in every other i-e-e-e seven fifty-four language. For money or any context where exact decimal arithmetic matters, reach for big-decimal.

In code, picture this. Bind val almost-max to integer dot max value. Print it, you see two billion one hundred forty seven million. Add one and print again, you see the same magnitude but negative — it wrapped around without error. Promotion is one-directional. A small integer widens to long or double automatically. A long does not narrow to integer automatically — you have to call dot to-int explicitly. And the floating-point reality. Print zero point one plus zero point two and you see the long tail. Construct two big-decimals from quoted strings, add them, and you see exactly zero point three.

String and the built-in interpolators.

A string in Scala is a java lang string — exact same class, exact same methods. There is no separate Scala string type. What Scala adds is a small set of string interpolators — prefixes you put before the opening quote that change how the literal is parsed.

The three built-in ones. The letter s before a string splices expressions with dollar name and dollar braces. The letter f before a string splices with print-f-style format specifiers attached. The letters r-a-w before a string splices like s, but does not process backslash escapes, so backslash-n stays as two characters.

There is also the triple-quote form, three double quotes opening and closing, for multi-line strings that contain quotes, backslashes, and newlines without any escaping. It composes with the interpolator prefixes — you can write s, three double quotes, content, three double quotes, and it just works.

In code, picture a val name bound to the string ganesh and a val score bound to ninety-two point three four five. Then s of hello dollar name prints hello ganesh. S of length equals dollar braces name dot length prints length equals six. F of score colon dollar score percent dot two f prints score colon nine two point three five — rounded to two decimal places. F of name colon dollar name percent minus ten s, pipe, score colon dollar score percent six dot two f — that produces a padded line with the name left-justified in a ten-character field and the score in a six-character field with two decimals. R-a-w of line one backslash n line two prints the literal characters line one backslash n line two, no newline. S of the same expression prints line one, newline, line two.

Triple-quoted multi-line strings are useful for s-q-l queries and embedded text. You bind a val sql to a triple-quoted string starting with select id comma amount, on the next line pipe from transactions, on the next line pipe where customer equals quote dollar name quote. The pipes are margin markers. Then you call dot strip-margin on the whole expression, and the leading whitespace up to and including the pipe is stripped off each line. The default margin character is pipe; you can pass a different one if your string contains pipes.

What interpolators compile to. An interpolator is a method call on the string-context class. When you write s of hello dollar name, the compiler rewrites it to string-context of the parts before and after the splice, followed by dot s of the spliced value. This means interpolators are not magic — you can write your own by extending string-context. Notebook eight revisits this.

Boolean, char, unit, null, nothing.

The non-numeric value types and the strange residents at the edges of the hierarchy.

Boolean is true and false. Same as java's boolean, backed by a one-byte primitive.

Char is a sixteen-bit unsigned integer interpreted as a u-t-f sixteen code unit. Written with single quotes — for example, single quote, a, single quote, or single quote, backslash n, single quote, or single quote, backslash u zero zero e nine, single quote. Because it is sixteen bits, characters outside the basic multilingual plane — most emoji, some chinese-japanese-korean extensions — require two chars to represent, a surrogate pair. Practical consequence. The length of a string containing a single emoji is two, not one. For real unicode handling, work with string and use code-point methods.

Unit is the type with exactly one value, written as empty parentheses. Used as the return type of methods that exist for their side effects. Equivalent to java's void, except unit is a real type you can write down.

Null is the type of the literal null. Subtype of every reference type. Modern Scala 3 setups can opt into explicit nulls with the compiler flag dash y explicit-nulls, which makes null no longer assignable to a string without an explicit nullable type. We will recommend option, from notebook six, instead — in every situation where you would otherwise reach for null.

Nothing is the bottom type. No values. The inferred type of throw, of the standard placeholder triple-question-mark for not implemented yet, and of an option of nothing in particular. Anywhere an expression of type nothing is used, the compiler accepts it as any type — because nothing is a subtype of everything.

In code, picture a boolean val flag set to true, then printing the negation of flag and false, which is false. Picture the char val letter set to single-quote a single-quote, then printing the length of a string containing one emoji, which prints two — one emoji, two u-t-f sixteen code units. Picture a unit method called log, taking a message, printing it with a prefix. Picture a val nothing-value bound to empty parentheses, the sole inhabitant of unit. Picture a method called not-done-yet, returning string, with body the triple-question-mark. This throws a not-implemented error when called, but the compiler is happy because the triple-question-mark has type nothing.

Type aliases.

A type declaration gives a name to an existing type. It does not create a new type — the alias and the original are interchangeable everywhere.

Two reasons to use them. Readability. A repeated complex type — say map of string to list of tuple of integer and double — becomes one named thing. Encoding intent. A user-i-d is conceptually different from a plain string, even when its representation is identical. The alias signals that.

When you need real nominal separation, where the compiler refuses to let you pass a plain string where a user-i-d is expected, reach for an opaque type or a value class. Notebook seven covers both.

In code, picture type user-i-d equals string, and type customer-map equals map of user-i-d to list of string. Then a function called lookup that takes a customer-map and a user-i-d, returning a list of string. Inside, it calls map dot get-or-else with the i-d and an empty list. You build a sample customer-map with two users mapped to invoice lists, and lookup returns the right list. Then to make the transparency visible, you bind a raw string to the value u one, then bind an i-d of type user-i-d to that raw string. No conversion is needed. The comparison i-d equals raw is true — they really are the same type at runtime.

The big shift — almost everything is an expression.

In imperative languages like python or java, if is a statement. It controls flow but does not have a value. To get a value out, you assign inside both branches.

In Scala, if is an expression. It evaluates to a value, and you bind that value with a val. The same is true for for-yield, for block expressions, and for pattern matching, which we will meet in notebook six.

This is a small mental shift with large downstream consequences for how Scala code reads. You stop writing — do this, then assign that — sequences. You start writing — the value of this is the result of choosing between these alternatives.

The one big exception is while. It is a statement-only loop that always evaluates to unit. We will see that next.

In code, picture this. Val score equals seventy-three. Val grade of type string equals an if-else-if chain — if score greater than or equal to ninety then the string a, else if greater than or equal to seventy-five then b, else if greater than or equal to sixty then c, else f. Print grade and you see c. Picture a for-yield. Val squares of type list of integer equals for n drawn from list of one through five, yield n times n. Print squares and you see one, four, nine, sixteen, twenty-five. Picture a for-yield with a filter. For n drawn from the same list, with the guard if n modulo two equals zero, yield n times n. Print and you see four and sixteen. Picture a block. Val area equals a block containing val r equals three, val pi equals three point one four one five nine, and the last line pi times r times r. The value of the block is the value of its last expression. Print area and you see the area of the circle.

While — the one statement-only construct.

While, and its sibling do-while, is the exception that proves the rule. It always evaluates to unit. There is nothing useful to return from a loop whose only purpose is to iterate by side effect.

Idiomatic Scala uses while sparingly — mostly in tight numeric loops where the alternative, a for-each call or a recursive call, would have measurable overhead. For everything else, reach for for-yield, or map, filter, fold, or recursion. Notebook four covers the higher-level alternatives.

In code, picture a var i bound to zero. A while loop with condition i less than three, body that prints iteration i and increments i. Bind val loop-result to the while expression, and you see that loop-result is the empty parentheses, the sole unit value. The same thing, expression-style, without var or while, is a range zero until three followed by dot for-each printing each element. Or if you wanted a value out, map over the range producing an indexed sequence of strings, no mutation at all.

Operators are method calls.

There are no operator declarations in Scala. What looks like an operator — plus, minus, star, less-than, equals-equals, even the cons-cell operator double-colon and the list-concat operator double-plus — is a method call written in a special syntactic position.

The rule. If a method takes one argument, you can call it without the dot and the parentheses. So one plus two is exactly one dot plus of two. And list one double-plus list two is exactly list one dot double-plus of list two.

This is why Scala lets you define methods with symbolic names. A class can declare a def plus method, taking another instance, returning a new instance, and the call site uses it like a built-in operator. Notebook five will use this to add operators to domain types.

In code, picture print line of one plus two — three. Print line of one dot plus of two — also three. Same thing. Print line of three dot less-than of five — true. Print line of list of one dot equals equals of list of one — also true, because list defines structural equality on its elements. Then picture a final class called money, with a primary constructor taking val cents of type long. Inside the class, def plus takes another money and returns a new money with the summed cents. Override def to-string returns an f-string formatting the dollars and cents. Bind val a to money of five hundred cents and val b to money of one hundred twenty-five cents. The expression a plus b uses the operator method you defined, and prints as dollar sign six point two five.

Type ascription — expression colon type.

A type ascription tells the compiler — evaluate this expression as this type. It does not change the runtime value. It changes how the compiler treats the value for subsequent inference.

Three honest uses. Forcing widening. Val xs equals list of one, two, three is list of integer. Val xs equals the same expression, then colon list of any, is list of any. The runtime value is identical. Disambiguating overloaded methods. When two overloads differ only by argument type, ascribing the argument picks one. Signalling intent on a return. Inside a long block, an ascription on the final expression documents the type the block is expected to produce, and the compiler enforces it.

In code, picture val ints of type list of integer equals list of one, two, three. Picture val anys of type list of any equals the same expression, then colon list of any. Both print as list of one, two, three. Then picture this — type ascription is not a cast. It only succeeds when the expression is already a subtype of the ascribed type. Val s of type string equals hello. Val a of type any equals s — that is implicit widening, always works. Val b of type any equals s colon any — explicit ascription, also works. But val c of type integer equals s colon integer — the compiler refuses, because string is not an integer.

What is next.

You now have the building blocks. How to name a value. What types are available. How literals widen and promote. How to splice expressions into strings. And the most important shift — that if, for, and blocks are expressions that have a value.

Notebook three builds on this by treating functions the same way. A function is a value — you can pass it as an argument, return it from another function, store it in a list. You will meet def, the method form. The function-value form, written as parameter list, arrow, body. The difference between the two. And the syntactic features that make Scala's function-rich style readable — named arguments, default parameters, by-name parameters, currying, and partial application.
